0 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186560349 #edb/ref 13194139543401 #edb/ref 17592186576411 false] [#edb/ref 17592186172130 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186730693 #edb/ref 13194139543401 #edb/ref 17592186576411 true] [#edb/ref 17592186172131 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186540197 #edb/ref 13194139543402 #edb/ref 17592186576411 false] [#edb/ref 17592186172131 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186730694 #edb/ref 13194139543402 #edb/ref 17592186576411 true] [#edb/ref 17592186175987 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186519842 #edb/ref 13194139543451 #edb/ref 17592186576411 false] [#edb/ref 17592186175987 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186731035 #edb/ref 13194139543451 #edb/ref 17592186576411 true] [#edb/ref 17592186176023 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186562815 #edb/ref 13194139543452 #edb/ref 17592186576411 false] [#edb/ref 17592186176023 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186731036 #edb/ref 13194139543452 #edb/ref 17592186576411 true] [#edb/ref 17592186176024 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186563859 #edb/ref 13194139543454 #edb/ref 17592186576411 false] [#edb/ref 17592186176024 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186731037 #edb/ref 13194139543454 #edb/ref 17592186576411 true] [#edb/ref 17592186179712 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186708254 #edb/ref 13194139543403 #edb/ref 17592186576411 false] [#edb/ref 17592186179712 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186730695 #edb/ref 13194139543403 #edb/ref 17592186576411 true] [#edb/ref 17592186180088 #edb/ref 1074 "A streamline must be tangent to the vector field at every point. In the first quadrant, the arrows point down and to the right. They become more horizontal near the $x$-axis and more vertical near the $y$-axis. The other quadrants have the corresponding reflected pattern.\n\nThe matching diagram therefore has hyperbola-shaped branches approaching the coordinate axes in all four quadrants. Along each branch, the tangent has the direction shown by the field.\n\nThe horizontal and vertical line families fail where the arrows are diagonal. The parabolas have horizontal tangents on the $y$-axis, where the field is vertical. The ellipses have vertical tangents on the $x$-axis, where the field is horizontal. None of these families matches the field." #edb/ref 13194139543422 #edb/ref 17592186576411 true] [#edb/ref 17592186198361 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186539392 #edb/ref 13194139543404 #edb/ref 17592186576411 false] [#edb/ref 17592186198361 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186730696 #edb/ref 13194139543404 #edb/ref 17592186576411 true] [#edb/ref 17592186198373 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186539356 #edb/ref 13194139543405 #edb/ref 17592186576411 false] [#edb/ref 17592186198373 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186730697 #edb/ref 13194139543405 #edb/ref 17592186576411 true] [#edb/ref 17592186198374 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186521963 #edb/ref 13194139543407 #edb/ref 17592186576411 false] [#edb/ref 17592186198374 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186730698 #edb/ref 13194139543407 #edb/ref 17592186576411 true] [#edb/ref 17592186198389 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186533081 #edb/ref 13194139543455 #edb/ref 17592186576411 false] [#edb/ref 17592186198389 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186731038 #edb/ref 13194139543455 #edb/ref 17592186576411 true] [#edb/ref 17592186198398 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186541345 #edb/ref 13194139543408 #edb/ref 17592186576411 false] [#edb/ref 17592186198398 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186730699 #edb/ref 13194139543408 #edb/ref 17592186576411 true] [#edb/ref 17592186198399 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186545110 #edb/ref 13194139543409 #edb/ref 17592186576411 false] [#edb/ref 17592186198399 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186730700 #edb/ref 13194139543409 #edb/ref 17592186576411 true] [#edb/ref 17592186367729 #edb/ref 1071 "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9,\\hspace{0.222em}y\\le 0,\\hspace{0.222em}y\\le z\\le 6\\},\n$$\n\nthen $$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139543436 #edb/ref 17592186576411 false] [#edb/ref 17592186367729 #edb/ref 1071 "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9,\\hspace{0.222em}y\\le 0,\\hspace{0.222em}y\\le z\\le 6\\},\n$$\n\nthen $$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+7}}\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139543436 #edb/ref 17592186576411 true] [#edb/ref 17592186367729 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/cc/ccc5cab37aed89c0d701ce0195414796211ddc73a498eaaa1ff145a45c42e9fd.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(3,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=6.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -3)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} & =3 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -6r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +9+{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )-6r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-6r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-6\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}0\\le r\\le 6\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\hspace{0.222em}r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le z\\le 6\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}6\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }}}{\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}}^{6}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z+1}}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139543436 #edb/ref 17592186576411 false] [#edb/ref 17592186367729 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/cc/ccc5cab37aed89c0d701ce0195414796211ddc73a498eaaa1ff145a45c42e9fd.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(3,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=6.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -3)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -6r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +9+{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )-6r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-6r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-6\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}0\\le r\\le 6\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\hspace{0.222em}r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le z\\le 6\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+7}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+7}}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}6\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }}}{\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}}^{6}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z+7}}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139543436 #edb/ref 17592186576411 true] [#edb/ref 17592186367731 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}6\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }}}{\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}}^{6}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z+1}}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta" #edb/ref 13194139543436 #edb/ref 17592186576411 false] [#edb/ref 17592186367731 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }